<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    GoogleIntegration's cross-section surface, carved into its own leaf project because the
    section is referenced in both directions: it consumes Humans.Teams.Contracts,
    Humans.Auth.Contracts, Humans.AuditLog.Contracts, Humans.Email.Contracts,
    Humans.Notifications.Contracts and Humans.SystemSettings.Contracts, while Teams,
    Notifications, Surveys and Monitor inject its services. A Contracts/ *folder* inside
    Humans.GoogleIntegration would therefore cycle at the assembly level (design §15.5b,
    memory/architecture/section-project-cycle-fix.md) — and would cycle a second way, because
    Humans.Application's IGoogleGroupSync and Humans.Infrastructure's HumansMetricsService
    consume it from Base — re-derived at G5 lane 5b-4 (nobodies-collective/Humans#866), which
    settled the last of the three names this line used to list: AuditViewerService went to
    Humans.AuditLog, SystemTeamSyncJob to Humans.Teams, and SuspendNonCompliantMembersJob's
    Google fan-out is now NonCompliantMemberSuspension inside Humans.Users.

    It holds exactly the eight interfaces something outside the section injects, plus the flat
    projections their signatures name. The rest — the Google API connector abstractions
    (IGoogleDirectoryClient, IGoogleDrivePermissionsClient, ITeamResourceGoogleClient, …),
    IGoogleAdminService, IGoogleWorkspaceUserService, ISyncSettingsService and
    IGoogleRemovalNotificationService — have no consumer outside
    Humans.{Application,Infrastructure,Web} and stay with the section.

    Two absences are load-bearing. ISystemTeamSync is NOT here: it syncs system-team
    membership, names Google nowhere in its signature, and its implementation
    (SystemTeamSyncJob) is Teams'. G5 lane 5c put it on Humans.Teams.Contracts, so Auth,
    Camps, Development, Governance and Onboarding reference that leaf rather than this one.
    And IDriveActivityMonitorService is not here because it moved to Humans.Monitor for the
    same reason, one lane earlier.

    IGoogleGroupSync came here at G5 lane 5c, from Humans.Application. Its only consumer
    outside this section is Teams' SystemTeamSyncJob, which already referenced this leaf.

    GoogleWorkspaceOptions came in the same lane: Camps' CampRoleService and Users'
    ProfileController both bind it, so it cannot be internal to the section.

    G5 lane 4b-2j moved the section's own vocabulary onto this leaf: the three EF entities
    (GoogleResource, GoogleSyncOutboxEvent, SyncServiceSettings) and five enums/constants
    (GoogleResourceType, DrivePermissionLevel, SyncMode, SyncServiceType,
    GoogleSyncOutboxEventTypes). They are here rather than under Humans.GoogleIntegration/Domain/
    because IGoogleSyncService and IGoogleSyncOutboxService expose the first two as public
    members and a leaf cannot reference its own section project — the same forcing move that put
    the nine Users entities on Humans.Users.Contracts. GoogleIntegrationDbContext maps them from
    the section project, which references this leaf; the tables, columns and migrations are
    untouched (namespace-only change, verified: has-pending-model-changes reports none).

    SyncAction lagged the rest of that set by one lane and is here now (G5 lane 3b). Lane 4b-2j
    moved it and was reverted on a Codex P1 against peterdrier/Humans#1310, on the grounds that
    it is a parameter type of IGoogleGroupSync.ReconcileOneAsync, which
    HangfireGoogleGroupSyncScheduler enqueues and schedules, and Hangfire serializes parameter
    types as assembly-qualified names. Peter's ruling, 2026-08-15: that premise is dead.
    ReconcileOneAsync is fire-and-forget/delayed and never a recurring job, so no recurring-job
    id is affected and RecurringJobExtensions needed no 'retired' entry; the only exposure is a
    job already queued at the moment of deploy, which fails visibly into Hangfire's Failed list
    and re-converges on the next reconciliation. See the remarks on the enum itself.

    References Humans.Interfaces (IApplicationService, ExternalWriteAttribute) and nothing else.
    The old Humans.Domain edge is gone with the project itself (lane 3b):
      - GoogleSyncSource — did not come here. It keeps the Humans.Domain.Enums namespace inside
        the Humans.Interfaces assembly, because Humans.AuditLog.Contracts names it in
        IAuditLogService's signature and Base's AuditEvent names it too; Base and another leaf
        cannot reach this one, so a shared home below both is the only place it fits.
      - GoogleEmailStatus — went to Humans.Users.Contracts with the rest of the UserInfo object
        graph. It was named here in a <see cref> only, so no reference was needed.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Humans.Interfaces\Humans.Interfaces.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="NodaTime" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
